# Angular patterns

Idiomatic component/service/change-detection design for current Angular,
and the anti-patterns to avoid when narrowing the common architecture rules
to this stack.

## Change detection

- Default new components to `ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush`. With
  signal-based state this is nearly free: reading a signal in a template
  registers it as a dependency and Angular schedules the check for you, so
  `OnPush` plus signals rarely needs a manual `markForCheck()` at all.
- If a component still receives data via `@Input()` mutation of a plain
  object (not a signal) under `OnPush`, replace the mutation with a new
  object reference (or migrate the input to a signal) — `OnPush` only
  re-checks on a new reference/signal change, not a mutated property on the
  same object.
- Reach for `ChangeDetectorRef.markForCheck()` only when a value changes
  outside Angular's own change-detection triggers (a third-party callback,
  a native DOM event Angular didn't bind) and cannot reasonably be modeled
  as a signal — not as a routine fix for "the view didn't update".

## Dependency injection and service boundaries

- Scope a service with `providedIn: 'root'` for app-wide singletons; scope
  it in a route's providers (or a standalone component's `providers`) for
  state that should reset per-feature/per-route instead of leaking across
  the whole app's lifetime.
- Keep a component's class focused on view state and event wiring; move
  business logic, HTTP calls, and cross-component state into an injectable
  service. A component `inject()`-ing `HttpClient` directly to build
  request logic inline is a sign that logic belongs in a service instead.
- Use Angular's `HttpClient` (with `provideHttpClient()` and interceptors
  for cross-cutting concerns like auth headers) rather than a bare `fetch`
  scattered through components — interceptors are the idiomatic place for
  auth/error handling, not per-call boilerplate.

## RxJS and signal interop

- Prefer `toSignal()`/`toObservable()` (`@angular/core/rxjs-interop`) at
  the boundary between RxJS streams (HTTP, router events, form
  `valueChanges`) and signal-based component state, rather than manually
  subscribing into a plain field.
- When an `Observable` genuinely needs a manual `.subscribe()` inside a
  component (rare once `async` pipe / `toSignal()` cover the common case),
  pipe through `takeUntilDestroyed()` (call it in an injection context, or
  pass an explicit `DestroyRef`) instead of hand-rolling a `Subject`+
  `ngOnDestroy` teardown.
- Do not fan a single source Observable out into multiple independent
  `.subscribe()` calls that each re-trigger the source's side effects
  (e.g. a re-fetching HTTP call); share it (`shareReplay`, or convert once
  with `toSignal()`) so the side effect runs once per meaningful change.

## Forms

- Prefer Angular's typed Reactive Forms (`FormGroup<{...}>`,
  `FormControl<T>`) over template-driven forms for anything beyond a
  trivial single-field form — typed forms catch a renamed/removed control
  at compile time instead of a silent runtime `undefined`.
- Validate with Angular's built-in or custom `ValidatorFn`s registered on
  the control, not ad-hoc validation logic scattered through submit-button
  click handlers.

## Anti-patterns

- Do not reach into a child component's internals via `ViewChild` to call
  a method or set a field when an `@Input()`/`input()`/signal binding, or
  an `@Output()`/`output()` event, would express the same interaction
  declaratively.
- Do not perform DOM manipulation directly through `ElementRef.nativeElement`
  in ordinary component code — use Angular's `Renderer2`, template
  bindings, or a directive when direct DOM access is genuinely required
  (a third-party widget, focus management); bypassing Angular's rendering
  layer breaks server-side rendering and change-detection assumptions.
- Do not put HTTP calls or other side effects inside a `computed()` — a
  computed's callback can re-run any number of times and must stay pure;
  side effects belong in `effect()` or an explicit event handler.
